      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "There is precedent for this on other social platforms:\n\n---\n\nYoutube Video:\nAudience can see: View count, Likes, Dislikes\nAuthor can see: View count, Likes, Dislikes, Retention, watch time, demographics, traffic source, share activity, etc.\n\n---\n\nTwitter post:\nAudience can see: Likes, Retweets\nAuthor can see:  Likes, Retweets, Views, Link Clicks, Profile Clicks, Detail Expands\n\n---\n\nFacebook Page:\nAudience can see: Likes\nAuthor can see: Likes, Followers, Views, Actions, Share Activity\n\n---\n\nI believe part of the reason for this is to make content creators the first-class citizen when it comes to knowledge about their own content. The consumer gets to consume the content - the author has a more privileged status in regard to their own work, enjoying the benefit of privately measuring the consumer's engagement, analyzing it, and using it to make decisions about the content.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2019-03-11T04:20:30.299Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "The new world statistics (Favorites, Total Visits, etc) are great information - for world creators to have. These numbers are not necessarily appropriate to share with the general public. It is standard practice among a lot of content platforms to consider this sort of information private.",
